# A template of a script: the fixture harness runs it stepped
# (`fixture-test`; docs/dev-practices/headless-mode.md, "How the gate runs a
# fixture"), with its setup (`Editor.Test.Fixture.Setup`): `tree/` is copied
# into the file fake's model under `/tmp/working-set-search.stepped0`, which
# `@DIR@` stands for, a path of the model with the byte length of the gate's
# `mktemp` template, and git, if asked, answers as outside a repository.
# The run is against the config in `../config/`, of which it loads nothing:
# there is no `reload`, so every command its keys run is one the editor
# ships. It must print exactly `expected` once the directory is written
# `@DIR@` and the job lines are left out, as the CLI's run left them out: a search's deliveries are what the library has found when the job
# reads it, so how many there are, and so how many job lines come of them,
# differs from run to run. A scene line carries no count of the scenes
# published, so the scenes that come of them change nothing here. It
# writes no file: the headless mode has no state directory here,
# so the working set is kept in memory alone.
#
# It is a working set's range added from project search's results: the
# tree is a workspace by its marker `.project`; `needle` is in two of its
# files, `a.txt` and `b.txt`, and not in `c.txt`. A row of the results
# added to a working set is the file's range, not the results', so the set
# opened shows that row of `b.txt`, and visiting it shows `b.txt` at the
# row. It pins what project search and the working set do together,
# whatever carries each.

# `:e` opens `a.txt`; the load and the workspace's find are waited for.
key : Semicolon shift
commit e @DIR@/tree/a.txt
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene

# `project-search` with no pattern asks for one, labelled with the
# workspace of the buffer it was called from; `needle` is searched, and the
# results show the excerpts of `a.txt` and `b.txt` in walk order, the
# search's matches drawn in `search-match`. `b.txt`, open in no buffer, is
# loaded by the search.
run project-search
join
scene
commit needle
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene

# The cursor goes down to the results' row of `b.txt`'s match, the
# seventh, and `working-set-add` asks for a set; `found` is a new set, and
# the range it adds is `b.txt`'s third row, not the results' seventh.
key j KeyJ -
key j KeyJ -
key j KeyJ -
key j KeyJ -
key j KeyJ -
key j KeyJ -
scene
run working-set-add
join
scene
commit found
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene

# `working-set-open` asks for the set and shows its buffer: the range is
# held in `b.txt`, so its row is the one excerpt, and none is drifted.
run working-set-open
join
commit found
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene

# `RET` in the set's buffer visits the range: `b.txt` at its third row.
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene
